So the helper:

```cpp
enum class AssignmentDisposition { kDispatch, kKeepQueued, kPauseAutoAssignment, kReject };

inline AssignmentDisposition DispositionForAssignmentOutcome(fleet::AssignmentOutcome outcome);

// scan-level
struct DispatchScanDisposition {
  bool should_dispatch = false;
  bool keep_queued = false;             // 至少一个候选被 deferred
  bool pause_auto_assignment = false;   // 至少一个候选 indeterminate
  bool rejected = false;                // 至少一个候选被永久拒绝
  bool rearm_start_request = false;     // 需要重新武装启动请求（留队/暂停）
};
inline DispatchScanDisposition SummarizeDispatchScan(const std::vector<AssignmentDisposition>& dispositions, bool any_dispatchable);
```

Hmm — the tricky part: `ready.empty()` determines whether to pause/keep. Note the semantics: if there was an indeterminate candidate AND a deferred candidate but no dispatchable, indeterminate takes priority (pause). If one candidate was dispatchable, we dispatch.

I'll model it as: given a list of dispositions in scan order:
- find first kDispatch → dispatch it, stop.
- if none dispatchable: if any kPauseAutoAssignment → pause auto assignment (+ rearm); else if any kKeepQueued → keep queued (+ rearm); else (all rejected or empty) → nothing special.

That matches. And rejections are handled per-candidate inline (cancel + fail) even if a later candidate dispatches — the code does `continue` after rejection and keeps scanning. Good.

So a helper:

```cpp
struct DispatchScanPlan {
  // 需要派发的候选下标（-1 = 无）
  int dispatch_index = -1;
  bool pause_auto_assignment = false;
  bool keep_queued = false;
  bool rearm_start_request = false;
  std::vector<int> rejected_indices;
};
```

That's testable and exactly maps. Then dispatcher_node can use it... but the dispatcher loop does reservation between selection and dispatch, which depends on the candidate. Restructuring the loop to use a precomputed plan would change semantics (the reservation step happens for each candidate until one succeeds). So a "plan first then act" refactor would change behavior (currently, a candidate that fails lock reservation is skipped and the loop continues to next candidate — which is a 4th disposition: "reserved denied → keep queued").

So the actual per-candidate dispositions include lock reservation failure. Let me define the helper at the level of "what to do with this candidate":

```cpp
enum class CandidateAction { kDispatch, kKeepQueued, kPauseAutoAssignment, kReject };
```
where lock-reservation denial maps to kKeepQueued (mission stays queued). Currently reservation denial does `continue` and doesn't set saw_deferred/saw_indeterminate — meaning if ALL candidates fail on lock reservation, `ready.empty()` and neither saw_deferred nor saw_indeterminate → return without ReArmQueueStartRequest. Hmm, is that right? Look: on reservation denial, it says "**不占用启动请求**，等锁释放时重试" — so no re-arm; the lock-release path re-arms. OK.

So the summary logic must distinguish: keep_queued due to deferred (rearm) vs keep_queued due to lock denial (no rearm). Currently these are separate flags (saw_deferred vs nothing).

