**Core (pure)**: new module `core/topology/topology_mutation.hpp` (or `catalog/topology_mutation.hpp`):
```cpp
enum class EdgeMutationKind { kCloseEdge, kReopenEdge, kSetOneWay };
struct EdgeMutation { Kind kind; std::string from, to; std::string reason; };
struct StationAddition { Station station; std::vector<EdgeSpec> edges; };
struct TopologyMutationRequest { std::vector<EdgeMutation> edges; std::vector<StationAddition> stations; };
struct TopologyValidation {
  bool ok; std::string message;
  std::vector<std::string> errors;            // 全量错误，不只第一条
  std::vector<std::string> disconnected_stations; // 连通性失败的站
  std::vector<std::string> affected_mission_ids;  // 在途任务受影响
  std::vector<std::string> affected_edge_keys;
  StationCatalog candidate;                   // 变更后的目录
  std::string candidate_hash;                 // 变更后目录指纹（内容寻址）
};
TopologyValidation ApplyTopologyMutations(const StationCatalog& base, const TopologyMutationRequest&, const TopologyValidationOptions&);
```
Validation steps:
1. Each mutation references existing nodes (from/to must exist) — errors otherwise.
2. Apply mutations onto a copy (pure).
3. `ValidateStationCatalog(candidate)` (structural).
4. **Connectivity**: every station must remain reachable from a designated reference set. Full all-pairs is O(n²·E) but our graphs are small; do BFS from every station and check the station set forms one strongly connected component? For bidirectional graphs, one component. For one-way toggles, "strongly connected" is the right criterion: a station that can be reached but can't be left is a trap. Use: build directed adjacency; compute for the rooted BFS from station[0], then on reversed graph; any station not in both → unreachable/trap → error naming it. This is O(V+E). Good, deterministic, testable.
